WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.550
Hey David, won't Medicare for all killed jobs?

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:09.440
This is a really loaded question and I'm glad
to have the opportunity to talk about it.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:16.000
There's this imagery of killing jobs that
is a very political imagery to be quite honest

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.820
with you because the reality is that all of
the time, governments, federal government,

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:26.890
state governments, departments of the government,
whoever, all the time decisions are being

00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:31.720
made that have an economic impact and they
have an employment impact.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:36.530
Sometimes we're talking about thousands, we're
talking about a thousand new jobs a but we

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:42.560
lose 200 but there's some economic gain or
some social gain from that change.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:47.100
So number one, we've got to get away from
this imagery of quote killing jobs because

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:52.820
all the time as a country evolves, decisions
are made that create jobs and that eliminate

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:53.820
jobs.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:57.210
They cause firings, they cause layoffs, they
create new industries, et cetera.

00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:03.150
What we have to understand is that a lot of
those jobs that exist in the medical field

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:09.079
right now, which would be unnecessary and
thus would disappear under a medicare for

00:01:09.079 --> 00:01:15.869
all system, are not jobs that are actually
providing for the greater good.

00:01:15.869 --> 00:01:23.090
What I mean by that is if you consider medical,
uh, insurance, health insurance, fraud investigators,

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:29.609
medical, a billing and coding experts on the
insurance side, medical billing and coding

00:01:29.609 --> 00:01:32.149
experts on the hospital side.

00:01:32.149 --> 00:01:35.659
By the way, not all of them would go away
under payer, under many single payer systems,

00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:42.270
they remain all of this bureaucracy plus the
profit that is implicit in the system.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:44.680
That doesn't lead to better health outcomes.

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:46.999
It doesn't lead to lower costs.

00:01:46.999 --> 00:01:53.170
It's simply yes, providing work to the people
that are in those jobs and it is work that

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:59.439
is being paid for at the expense of people
who are buying availing themselves of healthcare

00:01:59.439 --> 00:02:00.439
services.

00:02:00.439 --> 00:02:05.200
So would some of those things be eliminated
if medicare for all work to be passed?

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.649
Yeah, some of them would.

00:02:06.649 --> 00:02:09.000
The economy would have to adjust in history.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:10.970
This has happened many, many times.

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:16.480
I give this example often, and I think it
applies here, think back to the development

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:17.760
of the automobile.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:23.790
The automobile was a much more efficient means
of transportation than horse and buggy carriages

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:25.250
or whatever you call them.

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:29.890
There were people who said this is going to
eliminate the jobs of the drivers.

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:34.659
A, I don't know what they were called of the
horse buggies.

00:02:34.659 --> 00:02:40.640
Would that be a reasonable reason not or not
to allow cars to be developed or to prevent

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:42.239
the development of automobiles?

00:02:42.239 --> 00:02:43.560
Of course not.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:50.269
Ultimately, those individuals no longer were
in demand in that role of horse buggy drivers.

00:02:50.269 --> 00:02:53.330
They were reintegrated into the workplace
in other ways.

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:55.780
Some of them probably suffered longterm unemployment.

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:57.040
That's true.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:02.260
But we need to stop falling for this idea
that any policy that will result in any job

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:03.719
loss is unacceptable.

00:03:03.719 --> 00:03:09.000
In fact, it's actually a very sort of center
right idea.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:14.200
I mean, there's this infamous video of Milton
Friedman arguing with some college kid, uh,

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:18.790
where the college kid says, shouldn't there
have been consequences for some car company

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:21.290
that had, uh, a car that was dangerous?

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:23.299
And Milton Friedman said, okay, well hold
on.

00:03:23.299 --> 00:03:26.739
So there was a negative consequence from the
car, from some kind of negligence.

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:31.409
Uh, how much should that car company have
paid to fix that problem?

00:03:31.409 --> 00:03:35.250
If the car costs 10 grand and it costs them
10 grand to fix their problems, should they

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:37.060
have been forced to do it?

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:42.629
What about should they have co paid $100,000
to fix a problem on a $10,000 car?

00:03:42.629 --> 00:03:49.640
At a certain point you get to this, uh, a
realization that there are going to be negative

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:51.930
consequences to doing the right thing.

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:55.989
And whether you're on the Milton Friedman
side of that or not, it doesn't actually matter.

00:03:55.989 --> 00:03:59.730
The point is every single day decisions are
being made.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:04.730
Policy is being effected where some jobs are
lost, there is some economic costs.

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:10.689
It has to be accepted in a country of 340
million people, anytime you make any kind

00:04:10.689 --> 00:04:16.150
of change, there will be people who gain and
people who lose right now the losers are people

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:18.000
who can't afford to pay for care.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.829
People who get substandard care, people who
are going bankrupt.

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:26.741
Because of medical bills and those who gain
are the for-profit insurance companies, the

00:04:26.741 --> 00:04:30.840
elected officials that perpetuate the status
quo in exchange for donations and whatever

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:31.840
else.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:37.520
Yes, some jobs won't be necessary under Medicare
for all the net benefit is gigantic and that

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:40.280
or some system like it is still the right
thing.

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:45.460
Even though there will be uh, jobs that will
no longer be necessary and we just have to

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:49.759
accept that and not fall for the right wing
talking point that it will kill jobs.

00:04:49.759 --> 00:04:53.139
And by the way, that is a, that is an under
the defense of talking point.

00:04:53.139 --> 00:04:56.639
When the right goes to Medicare for all will
kill jobs.

00:04:56.639 --> 00:05:00.930
They've often already conceded some of the
benefits of just such a system in terms of

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:03.870
access to care and in reducing costs.

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:05.301
That's why they retreat to it.

00:05:05.301 --> 00:05:10.340
And it is a very, very weak counterpoint to
some kind of nonprofit system.

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:13.080
The likes of which many countries have already
employed.

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:14.210
Let me know what you think.

